what we're going to do in this video is  explore the relationship between local  linearity at a point and  differentiability at a point so local  linearity is this idea that if we zoom  in sufficiently on a point that even a  nonlinear function that is  differentiable at that point will  actually look linear so let me show some  examples of that so let's say we had Y  is equal to x squared so that's that  there clearly a nonlinear function but  we can zoom in on a point and resume  sufficiently in we will see that it  looks roughly linear so let's say we  want to zoom in on the point 1 comma 1  so let's do that so zooming in on the  point 1 comma 1 already it is looking  roughly linear at that point and this  property of local linearity is very  helpful when trying to approximate a  function around a point so for example  we can figure out we could take the  derivative at the point 1 1 use that as  the slope of our tangent line find the  equation of the tangent line and use  that equation to a plot to approximate  values of our function around x equals 1  and you might not need to do that for y  is equal to x squared but it could  actually be very very useful for a more  complex function but the big takeaway  here at the point 1 1 it is displaying  this idea of local linearity and it is  also differentiable at that point now  let's look at another example of a point  on a function where we aren't  differentiable and we also don't see the  local linearity so for example let's do  the absolute value of x and let me shift  it over a little bit just so that we  don't overlap is what much all right so  the absolute value of X minus 1 it  actually is differentiable as long as  we're not at this corner right over here  as long as we're not at the point 1  comma 0 for any other x value it is  differentiable but right at  equals one we've talked in other videos  how we aren't differentiable there and  then we can use this local linearity  idea to test it as well  and once again this is not rigorous  mathematics but it is to give you an  intuition no matter how far we zoom in  we still see this sharp corner it would  be hard to construct the only tangent  line a unique line that goes through  this point 1 comma 0 I can construct an  actual infinite number of lines that go  through 1 comma 0 but that do not go  through the rest of the curve and so  they'll notice wherever you see a hard  corner like we're seeing it 1 comma 0 in  this absolute value function that's a  pretty good indication that we are not  going to be differentiable at that point  now let's zoom out a little bit and  let's take another function let's take a  function where the differentiability or  the lack of differentiability is not  because of a corner but it's because as  we zoom in it starts to look linear but  it starts to look like a vertical line  so a good example of that would be  square root let's say 4 minus x squared  so that's the top half of a circle of  radius 2 and let's focus on the point 2  comma zero because right over there we  actually are not differentiable and if  we zoom in far enough we see right at 2  comma zero that we are approaching what  looks like a vertical line  so once again we would not be  differentiable at two comma zero now  another thing I want to point out all of  these you really didn't have to zoom in  too much to appreciate that hey I got a  corner here on this absolute value  function or a two comma zero or at  negative two comma zero something a  little bit stranger than normal is  happening there so maybe I'm not  differentiable but there are some  functions that we don't see as typically  in a algebra precalculus or calculus  class but it can look like a hard corner  from a zoomed out perspective but as we  zoom in once again we'll see the local  linearity and they are also  differentiable at those points so a good  example of that let me to get rid of  some of these just so we can really zoom  in let's say Y is equal to X 